COROSYNC FLOW-CONTROL BUG -- DLF warm-up, run 1 (one direction, ingress on the master)
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-25 11:15-11:20 NZST (device clock 23:15-23:20 UTC 09-24), tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 (stack), IE520-sa same; x230 awplus_5.5.5_2-20260918-7
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, member 3 = Active Master),
        static LAG sa3 (port1.0.13 + port4.0.9) to IE520-sa; config as restored by
        Terrence at ~10:40 (bench_probe.py read MATCH against tb470.setup at 10:57).
Bug:    with `terminal monitor` on and DLF traffic crossing the stack link, the log fills
        with "corosync[...]: [IPC ] coroipcs.c:383 Enabling flow control for <n>" /
        "coroipcs.c:373 Disabling flow control for <n>". Repro text: "Connect an ixia port to
        each stack member; use Ixia to send DLF traffic into both stack members."
Plan:   (Terrence) 10 s at 100 Mbps, 10 s at 500 Mbps, 10 s at line rate of KNOWN unicast,
        then 10 s of real DLF at line rate into the master. Frames 1500 bytes on the wire
        (1496 built + FCS; the AW+ jumbo safeguard sits at 1508).

VERDICT: NOT REPRODUCED in this direction -- 822,501 DLF frames (984 Mbps, ~1000 Mbps on the
         wire) ingressing the master's port3.0.9 were flooded out of every vlan1 port,
         including across sa3 to IE520-sa, with zero loss, and the stack logged NOTHING.

SETUP
  tb eth3 (00f0.4d00.7718) -> master port3.0.9      sender (scapy-built pcap, tcpreplay 4.5.1)
  tb eth1 (00f0.4d00.7716) -> master port3.0.13     receiver A (same member)
  tb eth2 (00f0.4d00.7717) -> IE520-sa port1.0.2    receiver B (behind sa3 = stack members 1+4)
  known-unicast frames:  dst 00f0.4d00.7716 (eth1, learned)   UDP 5010 -> 9
  DLF frames:            dst 02:00:00:00:99:99 (never learned) UDP 5010 -> 10
  Gates before traffic: no storm-control lines in running-config; 0200.0000.9999 absent from
  the MAC table; `show log | include corosync` empty; `terminal monitor` confirmed
  ("% Warning: Console logging enabled"). Receivers: tcpdump -w /dev/null with a filter on
  eth3's MAC + UDP sport 5010; kernel drops 0 in every phase.

PHASES (device time = NZST - 12 h)
  phase                        window          sent     tcpreplay      wire      rx eth1   rx eth2
  known-unicast 100 Mbps       11:18:04-14     83,557   100.0 Mbps   101.6 Mbps   83,557        0
  known-unicast 500 Mbps       11:18:23-33    417,781   500.0 Mbps   508.0 Mbps  417,781        0
  known-unicast line rate      11:18:42-53    822,501   984.1 Mbps   999.9 Mbps  822,501        0
  DLF line rate (-> port3.0.9) 11:19:08-18    822,501   984.0 Mbps   999.8 Mbps  822,501  822,501
  (rates: 82,2xx pps x 1500 B; "wire" adds preamble + IFG. tcpreplay --topspeed = line rate.)

  Member 3 CPU rx (show platform counter sdma rxDmaPcktCnt, clear-on-read, background ~650
  per read): 101,947 (accumulated since the last read, before) / 2,467 (ramp) / 1,148 (DLF).
  So the DLF flood did not reach the master's CPU in any volume -- hardware flooding.

  Device log 23:18:04-23:19:35 UTC: only our own IMISH command echoes and the syslog-ng
  reload that `terminal no monitor` triggers. `show log | include corosync` and
  `| include flow control` after the run: nothing but the commands themselves.

NOTES
  - The wrapper's 260 s timeout killed the driver after `terminal no monitor` had been sent
    (23:19:33) but before its final log dump; the console was re-read clean at 23:20 and the
    device log covers the whole window, so nothing is lost.
  - tcpreplay: 984 Mbps of frame bytes is 1G line rate for 1500-byte frames. Two NICs at
    once were already measured at ~983 Mbps each on 2026-09-24.
  - Known-unicast at line rate reached eth1 100% and eth2 0% (correctly switched, not
    flooded); the DLF phase reached both 100% (flooded). That is the behaviour the repro
    needs; the corosync symptom did not follow from it in this direction.

NEXT (Terrence's plan): the same DLF ingressing a NON-master member -- eth2 -> IE520-sa ->
  sa3 lands on members 1 and 4 -- and then both directions at once (eth3 into the master and
  eth2 via sa3 into members 1/4), which is the closest this bench gets to "Ixia into both
  stack members". After the recable (a direct member-1 NIC), repeat with a member-1 ingress.
